<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  tools/entitlements.plist — the exceptions Python needs under the hardened
  runtime, for DEVELOPER ID distribution (direct download).

  Notarization REQUIRES the hardened runtime (codesign -o runtime). The
  hardened runtime then forbids several things CPython does as a matter of
  course, so an app signed without these entitlements passes notarization and
  then dies on launch, which is the worst of both outcomes.

  Each key below is here because something concrete breaks without it. Do not
  add a key speculatively: every entitlement is a security guarantee given
  back, and Apple asks about the unusual ones.

  allow-unsigned-executable-memory
      CPython writes and then executes memory it just wrote. ctypes closures
      and libffi trampolines both do it, and both are on the path for
      soundfile and anything else that binds a C library. numba, which this
      app ships, JITs by definition.

  disable-library-validation
      Library validation demands every loaded dylib be signed by the SAME team
      as the host, or by Apple. A PyInstaller bundle is full of dylibs shipped
      by NumPy, llvmlite, librosa and libsndfile that are signed by their own
      vendors or by nobody. Without this, the first `import` of a compiled
      extension fails.

      ⚠ THIS ONE MAY NOW BE UNNECESSARY, AND IT IS WORTH TEN MINUTES TO FIND
      OUT. sign_macos.sh re-signs all ~344 nested binaries with OUR identity
      before signing the app. Once they all carry the same team ID, library
      validation has nothing left to object to. It was written in when signing
      was theoretical and every dylib still carried a vendor signature.

      Test it, once, after the first signed build succeeds: comment this key
      out, rebuild, and let the signed startup self-test run. If the app
      starts, delete the key for good. Dropping it is a security improvement
      on its own — and it is the single biggest thing standing between this
      app and the Mac App Store (see below).

  If the signed app refuses to launch, the next two to try, IN THIS ORDER, are
  com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit and
  com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables. Add one, rebuild,
  test. Adding both at once teaches you nothing about which was needed.

  ── On the Mac App Store, and why nothing here forecloses it ────────────────

  Developer ID and the Mac App Store are PARALLEL pipelines, not a sequence.
  Different certificates (Apple Distribution, plus an installer certificate),
  an embedded provisioning profile, a .pkg instead of a .dmg, upload instead
  of notarization, and App Review instead of an automated scan. One codebase
  can feed both. Choosing Developer ID now costs nothing later, and this file
  would simply gain a sibling — entitlements-mas.plist — rather than change.

  What a Mac App Store build WOULD require, recorded now so nobody has to
  rediscover it:

  1.  com.apple.security.app-sandbox is mandatory. It is deliberately absent
      here, because Developer ID does not require it and an unsandboxed app
      reads a library folder with no ceremony.

      ⚠ An earlier version of this comment claimed a sandboxed Trellis could
      not read a user-chosen library folder at all, and called that "the
      product." That was wrong, and it mattered, because it read as a closed
      door. The sandbox grants access to any folder the user picks in an open
      panel (com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write), and
      SECURITY-SCOPED BOOKMARKS persist that access across launches. That is
      precisely the mechanism a library-folder app is supposed to use. The
      real cost is engineering, not permission: bookmarks are an AppKit API,
      so it means PyObjC work at the settings layer, and network volumes such
      as /Volumes/music need the same treatment.

  2.  Network access needs com.apple.security.network.client and
      com.apple.security.network.server, which are routine and granted freely.
      Peer sharing over a Cloudflare Tunnel is not itself a problem: cloudflared
      is a separate process the user installs, and this app never ships or
      launches it. Shipping it INSIDE the bundle would be the problem — App
      Review guideline 2.5.2 does not love an app that carries its own daemon.

  3.  disable-library-validation is the genuine blocker. App Review treats it
      as an exception needing justification, and often declines. See the note
      above: it may already be removable.

  4.  allow-unsigned-executable-memory is broader than App Review likes;
      com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit is the entitlement that is normally
      accepted for the same need. If MAS ever becomes real, test whether
      allow-jit alone is enough for numba and ctypes.

  So the order of operations, if this is ever taken up: drop
  disable-library-validation, narrow the memory entitlement to allow-jit, then
  do the sandbox and security-scoped-bookmark work. The first two are cheap
  and improve the Developer ID build regardless. The third is a real project.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
